 {:seon.task/name "a-model-render-draws-each-turns-diff"
  :seon.task/plan "Stage 2b of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09: what the model reads is each entity's model render in the one walk, and each turn's page is generated from two points, never stored. Rewritten 2026-11-10 (Context) for what is now true.

Built, on jolt/model-wire-2a and carried by Web's jolt/pages (the pages-from-data step 1 branch), which lands it: the request is the walk's answer for :seon.render/openai-compat (seon.render/openai-compat, read by seon.ctx.wire/request). Each history entity's model render makes its native messages from its own pulled fields: seon.ctx.wire/agent-msgs (the system message, example sessions, the session's opening, its turns' entities placed by turn-entities), eval-msgs, message-msgs, model-call-msgs, defect-msgs (a model render that threw). A model render runs once a request, in its entity's own component, and a record placed for the model draws no html. The page reaches the model as calls Seon generates before each model call (seon.ctx.wire/page-diff-msgs): (my.render/page p) at the first turn, then (my.render/diff p0 p1), never stored, ids from the points. The parts at the turns' bases are drawn by one walk advanced along them (seon.ctx.wire/page-parts-through), reading the page's tiles (seon.render/page-pattern: the agent's own pattern less its history joins). A read made as a model's request (seon.turn/context-block) shows the page of the turn it opens (seon.ctx.wire/opening-page). seon.render/wire, show-page!, the stored page evals, page-value and changes-of are deleted. The wiki says so: docs/render.md#the-models-context-a-cached-prefix-then-the-turn and #the-render-is-an-eval.

Left: (1) the request cost proofs, waived (owner, 2026-11-10, docs/decisions.md), measured 2026-11-11 on main 69b9e57bb6 by seon.cost/measure at two sizes (ticks and bytes an eval; the cold draw on LMDB as its proof runs): seon.render/draw cold 421k ticks, 1.35 MB, 802 calls an eval (bound 200 calls, 120 KB); seon.render.proc/pass-view 78k ticks, 275 KB, 396 calls; seon.ctx.wire/request 10.8k ticks, 32 KB, 23 calls a record (bound 8 calls, 15 KB). Where the cold draw's goes: a kept page walk advances between two turns in 28 calls and reruns no component (seon.ctx.wire/page-parts-through keeps its walk: docs/render.md#a-page-is-a-kept-walk), so the per-turn cost is that turn's diff, seon.ctx/moved (~290k ticks on LMDB for ~20 datoms; Deep fixes is cutting it to ~94k in the Datahike fork: psi/diff's compare), and the records' model renders and prints (~130k). Found 2026-11-11 on main 72c9080cd7: that holds only while nothing flushes the walk between turns; a flush between turns (a :context read, a person's page, an eval's own) draws the pending turn's page at the db's own point, one transaction past the last basis, so the next turn's pair starts behind the kept walk and seon.ctx.wire/page-parts-through draws the page cold (~7.1M ticks, 31.6 MB; the :context read's 80 ms is this): fixed: the kept walk's slot holds the page at its last chain's first point, so that pair advances the walk (106k ticks, 15 calls at 10 turns, flat; seon.ctx.wire-cost-test/a-turns-pair-after-a-flush-costs-flat-in-turns); of the 802 calls, ~250 are ratom view plumbing (notify-deref-watcher!, wrap, view?, view), helpers production does not check per call, and ~300 the printer. The owner is deciding how cost is measured system-wide (Tests' page cost-measures-as-a-system-2026-11-11.md; Context's worked example of these three in the notes folder): a calls bound pushes work into uncounted helpers, so the bounds are to be restated in ticks and bytes by that decision; the waivers stay until then. Not to build without that gate and the owner's word: page messages kept across requests by their point pair (a second cache beside the walk's: AGENTS.md section 9). (2) Pages from data step 2 changes what the model reads once more: senders as refs, recorded_requests changed once, named. (The chat's order needed no change: it is when each record reached the branch, one order the page and the model share, docs/turn.md#the-turn-is-a-number.)"
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ctx.wire-test/the-recorded-requests-come-out-the-same}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-entity-is-drawn-by-its-schemas-render"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: open parts 7a (renders for section, result, var), 7b (four second renders merged) are two tasks in one (7c, layout-of deleted, is done with 2b), and it needs stage 1a on main and seon.repl/an-agent-draws-its-own-page merged; Web splits it into 7a/7b. Landed 2026-11-10 in jolt/batch-1110 (53b5f20b96): the one walk renders the outputs asked of it (`seon.render/render-pull`, `seon.render/openai-compat`), recognition by the best match (`seon.db/most-specific`, `seon.db/owners`), `seon.render.ui/agent-parts`, a search and a cite each drawn by their schema's render. Still open: (7a) renders for a doc section, a result and a var with its verdict, (7b) the four second renders merged, (7c) seon.render/layout-of and the layouts deleted: done with 2b (a walk is rooted on the entity's own render, `seon.render/render-var-of`). Stage 2a of docs/users.md#the-stages-agreed-2026-11-09 (owner, 2026-11-09: \"No layout-of. No seon.render/layout I want to remove all the manual layout work. A hiccup render can override anywhere we are walking and rendering recursively. It's just more data that we are detecting and rendering\"; \"Entities should have renders IFF we want them rendered\"). Needs stage 1a (a context keyed by attributes, on main) and seon.repl/an-agent-draws-its-own-page merged.

What's wrong: a page is drawn by hand-written layouts that decide in code which keys get parts and in what order (seon.repl/render-ctx, user-ctx, root-ctx, task-list, conversation-list, page-chrome's lists), named by :seon.render/layout and read by seon.render/layout-of; four entities have two renders (task: task-view and task/status/citations; model call: model-call-view and conversation-turn; eval: evaluation and eval-item/eval-glance; message: message and message-hiccup); a cite, a doc section, a search, a result and a var with its verdict have no render and are drawn inside layout code; a cite's pull reached back to its agent, drawn again (Web's lab, 2026-11-09).

The change: the walk's root is the pulled entity drawn by its render (its own :seon.render/hiccup, else its entity schema's); a render returns its data and the walk draws each entity it holds by that entity's render, one component each, keyed by its path (seon.render/emit: a tracking view of an entity placed in hiccup draws as its render's component, :at its path); an explicit [#'f {} x] stays for a true layout; a bare {:db/id} draws as a link to where the entity is, with a model render naming its identity value. One render per entity schema; the missing ones written; eval tiles: an eval whose result's value has a render (its own key, or :seon.eval/schema's) is drawn in the sidebar by the result's render. The page-or-conversation split is Context's model renders (stage 2b). Deletes: seon.render/layout-of, :seon.render/layout, the layouts and their lists, each second render. Proofs: a page is the user's (or an agent's) pull drawn with no layout var; a change to one entity reruns its component alone (seon.render-cost-test/a-change-costs-its-components, its bound measured before and moved only on the owner's yes); no entity is drawn twice; test/seon/recorded_requests.edn unchanged where the data is, else named part by part with Context.

Experiment A, 2026-11-09 (Web, a demo root on a clone of runs/store, lab renders over a ratom shaped like ctx: an agent, its task, three evals): a render that returns its data, with each view of an entity it holds turned into a call of that entity's schema's render, the view the call's argument (a six-line helper standing in for one branch of seon.render/emit; seon.ratom/view= makes the key stable), draws each entity as its own component keyed by its render and path, with no layout. Changing one eval's code reran that eval's component alone; the task's plan, the task's alone; an eval added, the new eval and the agent's list. Estimate held: O(the entity that changed), plus a lookup a member for the list it is in. So stage 2a's mechanism is that one branch of emit; the rest of the stage is deleting layouts and writing the missing renders.

The one-walk spike, 2026-11-09 (Web, branch jolt/one-walk at b823b4c71b, not for landing; src +140 -30). Owner: \"render both the hiccup and the agents context as the processing of the pull\"; \"The pull doesn't need to be in order the render functions for the data being processed can order them\"; \"look up what something is the same way our tx processor looks up and validates data\"; \"No magic functions\". Built and proved (tests one-walk-renders-a-pull-to-hiccup-html-and-messages and one-walk-a-turns-diff-is-rendered-not-stored, green):
- Recognition by the entity index's :entity-schemas (seon.db/index, the write check's), and one lookup naming an entity's render for every output (entity-render in the spike).
- In seon.render/render!, an entity's model render runs after its hiccup in the same run, reads tracked together; its output holds messages and the child entities it places, each the child's component (the one the hiccup placed, a cache hit), kept as a marker; the request is the root's output with each marker replaced by its child's, as seon.render/resolved does for hiccup (two lines).
- The agent's hiccup and model renders both order the turns, from one helper.
- One eval changed reruns its component alone, page and model; a new turn on a live walk reruns 3 components (the agent, the call, the eval) and leaves the earlier messages the same bytes.
Measured (seon.cost/measure, test world): a new turn costs O(turns): 0.65M ticks at 10 turns, 2.29M at 40, 9.17M at 160 (one turn added and taken back); the renders, the message read and the ctx diff are a seventh of it, the rest the walk's cache-hit path checking each earlier child, twice (hiccup and model output). Rendered from nothing (no cache): 1.5M at 10, 5.7M at 40, 22.8M at 160 (about 70 ms). So the cache is never relied on; a cheaper hit path (no contracted calls, the second placement reusing the first's key) is a later cost task, measured on a real session first.
To do here, beyond the spike: recognition by the best match (attributes held per schema, inherited counted, a tie an error value) instead of seon.db/most-specific's extends-all rule; the (and (map? v) (:db/id v)) check goes (a map under a ref attribute is an entity: the attribute it came through says what it reaches, the entity index's :targets); seon.db/parents, extends?, owners and their readers take the registry they read (the branch's, as seon.db/index-of gives the write check), where today they read the binary's while seon.contract/properties reads the branch's; renders named by data (a value's own :seon.render/hiccup, a schema's through seon.render/render-of) run as components, so seon.web.html/expand runs none and an eval's glance stops staying stale after its render is redefined; the spike's agent render becomes the agent's :seon.render/hiccup (seon.render.ui/agent's layout list goes).
It reds 11 tests: 10 because root's entity holds root's attributes and the seon.config.* keys (by the best match, the config): stage 3's config entity fixes the data; one, seon.render-test/one-path-to-the-model, holds the :wire parts' old law, which 2b deletes.

