 {:seon.task/name "a-style-on-a-var-no-drawn-namespace-holds-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Web (2026-11-11): the stage lost its CSS on main because its rules sat on private helpers. jolt/stagecss closed that case (the rules are read from ns-interns of every drawn render's namespace), but a var carrying :seon.render/style in a namespace where no render is drawn (a helper another namespace's render calls as a plain function) is still dropped with no word. What allowed it: a style is metadata any var may carry, and the collection reads only the namespaces of drawn renders. Correct, one of two, Web's pick first: a style sits only on a render (the schema's render or a var the render lookup names), so the contract refuses it elsewhere; or a derived class of ```seon.problems/health``` names each var with :seon.render/style that no drawn namespace holds. Proof: a styled helper in a namespace with no render is refused (or is a problem row naming it), and the wiki at main is none. docs/render.md states where a style lives."
