  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-render-that-answers-no-hiccup-is-said}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-joined-entity-of-a-registered-schema-reaches-the-page-and-the-request"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an entity a new schema declares, with both renders (:seon.render/hiccup and :seon.render/openai-compat), registered on a db as an agent registers one (my.schema/registration) and joined into the agent's own pattern (its :seon.ctx/pattern), is in the agent's pull and reaches neither the page nor the request (seon.render-test/a-joined-entity-of-a-registered-schema-reaches-the-page and a-joined-entity-of-a-registered-schema-reaches-the-request, red both; each lands with the fix that turns it green: a red test on main reds every batch). The cause, by the code path: seon.render/history? (src/seon/render.clj:1324) calls any schema with a model render and no pattern of its own the conversation, so seon.render/page-pattern (:1333) drops the join and seon.render.ui/tile? (src/seon/render/ui.clj:1890) places no tile; and the conversation is hand lists that never read the pull's joins: seon.render.ui/conversation-list (:2000, system message, examples, session open, ask, each turn, end, by var) and conversation-turn (:1975, a turn's messages, call and evals by attribute), and for the model seon.ctx.wire/agent-msgs (src/seon/ctx/wire.clj:599) and turn-entities (:522: task begins, messages, page diff, call, evals by attribute). agent-parts' tail (src/seon/render/ui.clj:1940) is the same kind of list for the agent's own state. (A second cause the registered-schema world found, two readers of a schema's properties, is gone: seon.render/schema-property reads props since jolt/oneprops.) The change: each hand list replaced by the pull's joins drawn by their schemas' renders, the conversation's members ordered as the data gives them (by transaction, docs/render.md#the-page), each through seon.render/entity-render for both outputs, so a joined entity reaches the page and the request with no code naming it. Deletes: the fixed vectors of agent-parts' tail, conversation-list, conversation-turn, agent-msgs and turn-entities. The two tests and the fix are on jolt/debuglabel (Web). Proof: the registered-schema-world test green, with no edit outside its data."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-joined-entity-of-a-registered-schema-reaches-the-page seon.render-test/a-joined-entity-of-a-registered-schema-reaches-the-request}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-part-says-what-it-left-out"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-11: 'Don't display the functions in full ... Agents need to know that context exists and we need explanations and short instructions that the agent can use to pull in more context'. What exists: a part that leaves things out says so in its own words or not at all, and the form that brings the rest is written by hand in some renders (docs/render.md#no-clipping). Plan: what every render an agent reads owes its reader, as data. A schema entry declares the key seon.render/more, pairs of words and a form in which `id` stands for the entity and `v` for the answer; the walk draws them once, for the person's page and the model's. A render that leaves things out answers `[:more {:count n :of words}]` and never a sentence of its own. First user: the namespace an agent stands in, shown as a cite its task makes of its own namespace (a cite may target a namespace: docs/references.md#an-entity-cites): the functions the task cites there, those they call and those the branch changed, one line each, the rest as a count; the dial is the cite's depth. Measured on eight tasks: 207 to 2,264 characters, where every function's first line was 9,547 on seon.render. An agent stays in its task's namespace (the owner's ruling). Deletes: each hand-written 'more' sentence as its render is moved. Proof, red first: over every part of a seeded agent's page longer than a new config key, a part's characters, the part either shows the whole or holds a count of what it left out and a form that answers it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-part-says-what-it-left-out}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-systems-session-presents-as-the-strategist"
  :seon.task/plan "PLAN part 3 (docs/plan.md), step 1 of the strategist note (notes folder, root-as-strategist-2026-11-11.md). What exists: the system's session is an agent keyed \"root\" working the parked chat task request/root, woken by each message to it, drawn by the stage (seon.render.ui/stage, given at boot, seon.system/root-session); at its REPL it reads its pull with my.user/agents, starts work with my.task/launch! and moves the person's view with my.user/focus!. What is missing: nothing tells it it is the strategist, so it answers as any agent does. The change: request/root's plan says what it is for (it answers the person, delegates by launch!, moves the view by focus!, and keeps its own answers short, since the stage shows them in large type), written as the task's data, and home opens the system's page when no focus is set. Proof: seon.render-test/a-request-on-home-is-answered-by-the-systems-session (a request typed on home reaches request/root's agent, whose reply is on the stage)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-request-on-home-is-answered-by-the-systems-session}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-focus-agents-result-is-on-the-systems-page"
  :seon.task/plan "PLAN part 3 (docs/plan.md), step 4 of the strategist note. What exists: a joined entity with a hiccup render is placed on a page by the tile rule (seon.render.ui/tile?), with no UI code naming it; the system's session's pattern (seon.system/root-session-pattern) joins the agents it started. Missing: a focus agent's result (an entity of its area's schema, joined from the agent) is not in that pull, so it is on no page but the agent's own. The change: the session's pattern joins what each started agent joins that has a render (a pattern clause, data), so the tile rule places it on the system's page. Waits on the focus agents themselves (Optimization). Proof: seon.render-test/a-focus-agents-result-is-on-the-systems-page (an area entity with a render, joined from an agent the session started, is drawn on the system's page with no UI code naming it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-focus-agents-result-is-on-the-systems-page}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-debug-page-labels-any-history-member-by-what-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by Web's review of the conversation's placement from data (2026-11-11): the conversation now places any history join's member (seon.ctx/placed-messages, the registered-schema world's note among them), but the debug page's label of each message the model was sent (seon.ctx.wire/part-ref) knows a message, an eval, a reply, a page and an example by their attributes and falls back to [:session key] for anything else, so the message of an entity whose schema the agent registered is labelled as the session's. The change: part-ref labels a placed member by its entity schema (seon.render/entity-schema) and its id, as the placement holds it, no attribute named. Proof: seon.render-test/a-placed-history-member-is-labelled-by-its-schema, beside the registered-schema tests whose world it uses (that world's note is labelled by its schema on the debug page)."
