  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-placed-history-member-is-labelled-by-its-schema}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-hits-definition-line-is-read-from-its-form"
  :seon.task/plan "Context's review note on branch jolt/citecall (2026-11-12). The search hit's part shows a function's arglist when the hit's line is only the opening of its own definition, and decides that by a regex over the line. The program db knows each var's form and its text, so whether a line is the first line of the var's own form is a comparison with that text, the compiler's fact, where the regex guesses at what a definition's opening looks like (def forms it does not list, metadata it does not expect). Plan: the hit's line is the definition's opening when it equals the first line of the var's form's text; the regex goes. Cost: the first line of one form's text, read only for a hit that has a line. Proof, red first: a hit on a var defined by a macro of another name (a contracted defn, a deftest) whose line is its form's first line shows its arglist; the regex misses it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-hits-definition-line-is-its-forms-first-line}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-render-that-throws-has-one-boundary"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Web. Found 2026-11-12 by Web's sweep for locks and guards (the owner's rule: a guard is likely a bug). One failure has two catches: render! (seon.render, the component-defect edge of docs/failure-points.md) catches a component that throws in the walk, and the html writer (seon.web.html/write!) catches again when it calls a function-headed node [f & args] after the walk has ended, writing the error as a bare span string, not as data: no problem is recorded and the model never sees it. The ordinary way is Reagent's: every [f & args] is a component that the render walks, so one error boundary sees every throw. Read (Web, 2026-11-12): the walk keeps a call whose render declares no input (seon.render/inputs-in empty) as written, its arguments realized, because the model reads that call as data (seon.render/emit's docstring: a call of the printer prints the value, and the model reads the call); so expanding it in the walk changes what the model reads, and making each such call a component adds a cache entry for every printed value. Decide with Context which before building. Plan: first read why the walk leaves a function-headed node to the writer; then expand every such node inside render!'s walk, so the writer gets only data; delete write!'s try/catch. Removes one catch and adds none. Mapping (the owner, 2026-11-12: Flow, channels, atoms, values): a render's output is an immutable value; the one catch is needed as the named edge where agent-written code meets the system. Proof to write, beside seon.render-test's component-defect tests: a component nested as [f] inside another's hiccup that throws is drawn by the :seon.render/component-defect render and is in take-defects!."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-view-defect-is-recorded-once-by-its-identity"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Web (seon.render.proc/view-pass). Found 2026-11-12 by Web's sweep for guards. A view whose render throws is held as its error page and recorded as a platform problem through {:op :error}. The branch (when-not (= e (:error held)) ...) skips sending the same error again, because recording it is a write that the view reads, which would walk the view again: a cycle broken by a guard. The view walks again only when a commit touches its reads (view-pass's docstring), not on every pass. The ordinary way: a fact is upserted by its identity, and Datahike adds no datom when a transaction asserts what already holds, so a repeat touches no read and walks nothing. Plan: record a view's defect as a fact keyed by its view (data first: Data model settles the key and its schema), so the same error again is a no-op transaction; delete the guard. Removes one guard and adds none. Mapping: the held error page is the render proc's Flow state; the problem is a fact, a value in the store; the guard goes. Proof to write: a view that throws, with an unrelated read then changed, records its defect once and is walked once more, not in a loop."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-render-walk-is-the-render-procs-state"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: Web (the render proc), with Context on the request side (seon.ctx.wire). Design: the notes folder's evidence/render-walk-design-2026-11-12.md (Point, 2026-11-12). Built (Web, jolt/web): seon.render/walk, a walk kept as a value: (walk prev {:db :tree :root :index :outputs :changes :moved :model-only}) answers the next prev, holding {:answers {output [[value source] ...]} :ran :defects} and its cache; no reaction, watch or lock, so its owner (an agent's process, the render proc) holds it and hands it back. A component is reused when its arguments are the same (a view by its path), its render the same fn, none of the datoms it read in :moved and none of the tree paths it read under the tree's diff (seon.ratom/reads-of, read-index, reached: the reactive walk's own index, as values). Measured at 40 turns (seon.render-cost-test/a-request-made-again-costs-what-moved): the same request again 3.3k ticks, flat; one eval more 1.11M (the request drawn cold, seon.ctx.wire/render, 2.4M), linear because the agent's model render lists every turn (seon.ctx.wire/a-turn-is-a-component-of-the-request); cold 4.3M (reads tracked). Left: (1) Context: seon.ctx.wire/render calls walk with the agent's own kept value (its tree patched by seon.ctx/patch, moved by seon.ctx/moved), and the kept-walk lock and its volatiles at seon.ctx.wire:370 go; (2) Web: the render proc's walks (:user-walks, :branch-reads :walk) become walk values in its returned state, no volatile inside it; (3) the reactive walker goes once nothing draws by it. Proofs: the reuse proofs below, seon.render-test, seon.render-cost-test, seon.ctx.wire-test and seon.ctx.wire-cost-test green with no cost bound moved, and the counts of the design in the tip."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.render-test/a-walk-kept-as-a-value-answers-what-a-cold-request-does seon.render-cost-test/a-request-made-again-costs-what-moved}}]
